// Lists, enumerations, and term lists.

--- list ---
- apples
- pears
│ <ul>
│ <li>apples</li>
│ <li>pears</li>
│ </ul>

--- list-nested ---
- apples
  - green
  - red
- pears
│ <ul>
│ <li>apples<ul>
│ <li>green</li>
│ <li>red</li>
│ </ul>
│ </li>
│ <li>pears</li>
│ </ul>

--- list-item-with-two-paragraphs ---
// One paragraph in an item reads as a plain item; several need marking off.
- #[First.

  Second.]
- Plain.
│ <ul>
│ <li><p>First.</p>
│ <p>Second.</p>
│ </li>
│ <li>Plain.</li>
│ </ul>

--- enum ---
+ first
+ second
│ <ol>
│ <li>first</li>
│ <li>second</li>
│ </ol>

--- enum-explicit-numbers ---
3. third
4. fourth
│ <ol>
│ <li value="3">third</li>
│ <li value="4">fourth</li>
│ </ol>

--- terms ---
/ apples: red
/ pears: green
│ <dl>
│ <dt>apples</dt>
│ <dd>red</dd>
│ <dt>pears</dt>
│ <dd>green</dd>
│ </dl>

--- terms-with-markup ---
/ *apples*: a _fruit_
│ <dl>
│ <dt><strong>apples</strong></dt>
│ <dd>a <em>fruit</em></dd>
│ </dl>

--- enum-explicit-start-continues ---
// A number the document wrote itself; <ol> carries on from it.
3. three
+ four
│ <ol>
│ <li value="3">three</li>
│ <li>four</li>
│ </ol>

--- list-item-with-inline-markup ---
- one with _emph_ inline
- two
│ <ul>
│ <li>one with <em>emph</em> inline</li>
│ <li>two</li>
│ </ul>
